statement.p:
Based on your knowledge of the behavior of the numerator and
denominator, predict the value of the limit
\displaystyle\lim_{x\to0^+}\frac{\sin x}{x^{1/2}}

\displaystyle\lim_{x\to0^{+}}\frac{\sin x}{x^{1/2}} =
\displaystyle\lim_{x\to0^{+}} [  /
 ] =

\lim_{ x \rightarrow \infty } \frac {\sqrt{ 10 + 2 x^2 }  }{( 8 + 11 x ) }
Enter  I  for \infty,  -I  for -\infty,
and  DNE  if the limit does not exist.

solution.p:
We  multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate radical:  
\begin{array}{rcl}  \displaystyle \lim_{ x \rightarrow \infty } 
\left( \sqrt{ x^2 + 3} - \sqrt{x^2 - 5} \right) 
\amp =\amp   \displaystyle \lim_{ x \to  \infty } 
\left( \sqrt{x^2 + 3} - \sqrt{x^2 - 5 } \right)    
\frac{\sqrt{x^2 + 3} + \sqrt{x^2-5}}{\sqrt{ x^2 + 3} + \sqrt{x^2- 5}}
\\ 
\amp =\amp   \displaystyle \lim_{ x \to  \infty }  \frac{(x^2 + 3) -  (x^2-5)}{\sqrt{ x^2 + 3} + \sqrt{x^2 - 5}}
\\  \amp  = \amp  \displaystyle \lim_{ x \to  \infty }  \frac{ 8}{\sqrt{ x^2 + 3} + \sqrt{x^2 - 5 }} 
\end{array} 
Notice that the denominator of this last expression 
\left(\sqrt{ x^2 + 3} + \sqrt{x^2 - 5 } \right) becomes large as 
x \to \infty (it's bigger than x). 
So 
\lim_{ x \rightarrow \infty } 
\left( \sqrt{ x^2 + 3} - \sqrt{x^2 - 5} \right)  =
\displaystyle \lim_{ x \to  \infty }  \frac{ 8}{\sqrt{ x^2 + 3} + \sqrt{x^2 - 5 }}    = 0

statement.p:
Finally, find all critical points in the graph of f(x)
(enter x values as a comma-separated list, or none
if there are no critical points):

Find the arc length of the graph of the function 
f(x)=7 \sqrt{x^{3}}
from x= 5 to x=6.

solution.p:
And the sum of lifting all of the slices from the bottom (x=0) to the top of the water level's depth (x=2) is
\begin{array}{cl}
\int_0^{2}(9.8)(1030)\left({\frac{16}{49}} \pi x^2\right)(7-x)\,dx  \amp  =10094\left({\frac{16}{49}}\right)\pi\int_0^{2} (7 x^2-x^3)\,dx \\
  \amp    =10094\left({\frac{16}{49}}\right)\pi\left[\frac{7 x^3}{3} - \frac{x^4}{4}\right]_0^{2}  \\
  \amp    =10094\left({\frac{16}{49}}\right)\pi\big[{\frac{56}{3}}-4\big]  \\
  \amp    =10094\left({\frac{16}{49}}\right)\pi\big({\frac{44}{3}}\big) \\
 \amp  \approx 151869\;\;\text{J}
\end{array}

statement.p:
Compute the value of the following improper integral.  If it is divergent, type "Diverges" or "D".
\int_{0}^{2}\! \frac{dx}{x^{2}-4x+3}
Answer:

solution.p:
Then, since \displaystyle\int \frac{r\sec^2{\theta}
\hbox{d}\theta}{(r^2(1+\tan^2{\theta}))^{(3/2)}} = \displaystyle\int
\frac{r\sec^2{\theta} \hbox{d}\theta}{(r^2(1+\tan^2{\theta}))(r^2(1 +
\tan^2{\theta}))^{(1/2)}} = \displaystyle\int
\frac{\hbox{d}\theta}{r^2\sec{\theta}} = \frac{1}{r^2} \sin{\theta}
using a trig identity.

statement.p:
Which of the given functions is a solution to \frac{dy}{dt}=k(A y-1)?




y = A + C e^{-kt}




y = A + C e^{kt}




y = -A + C e^{-kt}




y = -A + C e^{kt}




y = A^{-1} + C e^{Akt}




y = A^{-1} + C e^{-Akt}

Find an equation of the curve that satisfies 
\frac{dy}{dx} = 150 yx^{14} 
and whose y-intercept is 4.

